WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.673
Build the Recovery Plan Before You Need It. The best

2
00:00:04.673 --> 00:00:09.346
time to discover a hidden dependency is not during an

3
00:00:09.346 --> 00:00:14.019
outage, a deadline, or a difficult handoff. It is while

4
00:00:14.019 --> 00:00:18.692
the work is still calm enough to map. Start with

5
00:00:18.692 --> 00:00:23.366
one critical workflow. List the trigger, inputs, systems, credentials, human

6
00:00:23.366 --> 00:00:28.039
owner, output, archive location, fallback, and acceptable recovery time. Run

7
00:00:28.039 --> 00:00:32.712
a small recovery exercise. Ask one simple question: if this

8
00:00:32.712 --> 00:00:37.385
tool or person were unavailable today, what would we do

9
00:00:37.385 --> 00:00:42.058
next? Do not aim for a perfect business-continuity plan. Aim

10
00:00:42.058 --> 00:00:46.731
for an honest first answer. Keep the fallback usable. A

11
00:00:46.731 --> 00:00:51.404
fallback that has never been tested is only an assumption.

12
00:00:51.404 --> 00:00:56.077
Save the essential files, document the restart sequence, and make

13
00:00:56.077 --> 00:01:00.751
sure another responsible person can follow it. Turn resilience into

14
00:01:00.751 --> 00:01:05.424
routine. One workflow map per month is enough to create

15
00:01:05.424 --> 00:01:10.097
a record of what the organization truly depends on. The

16
00:01:10.097 --> 00:01:13.368
result is calmer operations and fewer surprises.
